(* ============================================================================
   16. FOOTNOTES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-025] (governs `footnote` / `footnote_definition`
      in PART 3). The reference form `[^label]` + `[^label]: body` and the
      INLINE form `^[content]` are implemented, and they are the only note
      forms: `[>` is unclaimed and `[>foo]` is literal text. Numbering, which definition wins,
      collection inside containers, unresolved references and unreferenced
      definitions are PART 9R R2 and CARVE-P9-034; the rendering rules below stay
      normative here.
      - The `[^label]: ` marker opens a marker body under §24 C3
        (CARVE-P9-083), with its content column two columns past the authored
        marker column. Its children are parsed as blocks. The body's distinct
        result is an endnote entry; its extent, blank runs, lazy continuation
        and `+` forms are the shared marker-body rules.
      - An unresolved reference keeps no trailing `{attrs}` in round trips.
      - Rendering (djot-compatible roles):
          reference -> <a id="{refId}" href="#fn{n}" role="doc-noteref"><sup>{n}</sup></a>
          endnotes  -> ONE <section role="doc-endnotes"> appended AFTER
                       all body content (outside heading <section>s),
                       containing <hr> then an <ol> of <li id="fn{n}">;
                       each note's last paragraph gets a trailing backlink
                       <a href="#{refId}" role="doc-backlink"
                          aria-label="{name}">↩</a>.
        First reference to note n has refId `fnref{n}`; a k-th (k>1)
        repeat uses `fnref{n}-{k}` and adds another backlink.
        The first backlink follows the final paragraph's inline content
        immediately, with no inserted whitespace. Separate later backlinks
        from one another with one ASCII space.
        INSIDE LINK TEXT A REFERENCE IS NOT A LINK, since links never nest
        (PART 3): it renders `<sup id="{refId}">{n}</sup>`, keeps its ordinal
        and its backlink, and its trailing `{attrs}` and
        `data-carve-footnote-label` go on that `<sup>`. So `[t[^1]][r]` renders
        `<a href="/u">t<sup id="fnref1">1</sup></a>`.
        An authored `{#id}` replaces the generated refId under PART 10 §1,
        on the `<a>` or, inside link text, on the `<sup>`. The backlink targets it. A reference without one keeps
        `fnref{n}` / `fnref{n}-{k}`, and the ordinals do not change. So
        `a [t[^1]{#z}][r] [^1]{#y}` renders `<sup id="z">1</sup>` and
        `<a id="y" href="#fn1" role="doc-noteref">`, and the note's
        backlinks are `href="#z"` and `href="#y"`. The
        backlink glyph is the plain return arrow `↩` (Carve's choice;
        djot appends a variation selector).
        THE BACKLINK CARRIES AN ACCESSIBLE NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-026]. With the
        `role` alone the NAME is the glyph, which a screen reader announces by
        its Unicode name or skips. `{name}` is the
        `footnoteBacklink` label (PART 9 §16a) followed by WHAT THE LINK VISIBLY
        SAYS -- the label alone for a lone backlink (`↩`, so
        `aria-label="Back to reference"`), the label plus k for the k-th of
        several (`↩<sup>k</sup>`, so `aria-label="Back to reference 2"`).
        Matching the visible text is WCAG 2.5.3, and it is why the number here
        is the REFERENCE ORDINAL and not the note number. The name is TEXT and
        is attribute-escaped.
        THE ENDNOTES SECTION CARRIES AN ACCESSIBLE NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-027]. The
        REGION the links sit in is named too. The section takes `aria-label` when it has no authored title. The string is
        the `endnotes` label (CARVE-P9-063), escaped the same way:
            <section role="doc-endnotes" aria-label="Footnotes">
        A placed `::: footnotes` with a quoted title instead takes
        `aria-labelledby` at that title's id under §12 [CARVE-P9-072].
        The default is "Footnotes" rather than "Endnotes" because that is the
        word the rest of this grammar uses for the construct. A host that
        renders them at the end and calls them endnotes sets the label; that is
        what the map is for.
      - A TOP-LEVEL `::: footnotes` THAT PLACES THE ENDNOTES SECTION -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-075]. When at least one note is referenced, the first
        top-level `::: footnotes` places the one endnotes section at its
        position. Render each block authored in that directive's `children`
        in source order immediately BEFORE the section, as sibling blocks in
        the surrounding document or heading section. The authored blocks are
        not children of the generated endnotes section. The opener's quoted
        title and `[label]`, when present, instead become the section's first
        children under PART 9 §12 [CARVE-P9-072], before its `<hr>` and `<ol>`.
        Later `::: footnotes` markers, and a marker in a document with no
        referenced notes, render the ordinary `<div class="footnotes">` floor
        of §12 with their own title, label, and authored children. A nested
        marker follows [CARVE-P9-073] below, even when it appears before the
        first top-level marker.
      - DOCUMENT-WIDE PLACEMENT MARKERS REQUIRE DOCUMENT TOP LEVEL -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-073]. A `::: footnotes`, `::: bibliography`, or
        `::: references` marker inside a block-level container -- a block
        quote, a list item, a div or directive body, a table cell, a definition
        description, a footnote definition -- does not place its document-wide
        region. It renders the `<div class="{kind}">` floor of §12 where it is
        written, and the region goes where it would without THIS marker. A
        processor with lint diagnostics reports `{kind}-placement-in-container`
        for the kind it supports. This rule does not restrict `::: toc`,
        `::: glossary`, or `::: index` inside a container.
      - A MARKER IN A TOC BODY RETAINS ITS CONTAINER SCOPE -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-076]. With the `toc` placement extension enabled, a
        document-wide placement marker inside its body remains subject to
        CARVE-P9-073. Render its div fallback before the generated `<nav>`,
        where the extension emits its authored body. Moving that body ahead
        of the nav does not promote the nested marker to document top level.
        Its region goes where it would without that marker, including the
        default position when there is no other eligible placement marker.
      - BACKLINK PLACEMENT WHEN THE BODY DOES NOT END IN A PARAGRAPH.
        Append backlinks to the note's last block only when that block is a
        paragraph. Otherwise synthesize a trailing sibling `<p>` after the
        last block. Never use a paragraph inside or before that block, even
        when a contained quote ends in one. A body consisting of one
        non-paragraph block also gets the synthesized paragraph.
      - A BODY WHOSE EVERY BLOCK RENDERS NOTHING IS AN EMPTY BODY -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-077]. Render the body's visible block elements on their own
        lines (PART 10 §4), then the synthesized backlink paragraph. If all
        blocks render nothing, the backlink paragraph is the `<li>`'s first
        line, on its own line inside it with no blank or slot line before it
        (carve#2570, corpus 528): comments, comment fences and
        target-mismatched raw blocks add no line.
        With visible and invisible blocks together, emit only the visible
        blocks' lines. This uses CARVE-P10-001's render-based emptiness test,
        but an empty note keeps no blank line because it has a backlink;
        an empty container keeps its blank line. Empty and all-invisible
        note bodies MUST serialize identically.
      - INLINE FOOTNOTE `^[content]` (pandoc form; a deliberate carve
        extension -- canonical djot has no inline footnotes). A `^`
        IMMEDIATELY before `[` opens an anonymous note whose content is the
        balanced bracket span (escape- and code-span-aware close, same as link
        text). Properties:
          * Content is INLINE-only, parsed recursively with footnote
            recognition DISABLED inside it (no `^[…]` or `[^ref]` nested in a
            note, either direction).
          * Numbered in the SAME document-order sequence as reference notes
            (an inline note always takes a fresh anonymous number; it cannot
            be re-referenced). Renders the same noteref + an `<li id="fn{n}">`
            in the one endnotes section, content as one `<p>` + backlink.
          * A trailing `{attrs}` attaches to the noteref `<a>`, or to the
            `<sup>` inside link text (like a reference note).
          * Empty or whitespace-only (`^[]`, `^[ ]`) is literal; an unclosed
            `^[…` is literal.
        PRECEDENCE (PART 8): `^[` opens an inline note, and a `^` not
        immediately before `[` opens none. Consequences:
        `^[x]^` is a note then a literal `^`; `\^[x]` is literal; a `^` not
        immediately followed by `[` is plain text.

   16a. ENGINE-WRITTEN LABELS are defined in the full PART 9 §16a clause below.

   17. TIGHT vs LOOSE LISTS + CONTINUATION MARKER -- OPERATIONAL (governs
      `list` rendering and the continuation_marker productions in PART 2).
      L1 LOOSE(list) := some item is followed by a blank line before the
         next sibling marker, OR some item holds a blank-line-separated
         second PARAGRAPH; otherwise TIGHT. The paragraph must be the item's
         OWN: a blank-separated second paragraph inside a container the item
         holds (a nested item, a block quote, a `:::` container or a
         description body) is that container's paragraph and does not loosen
         the item (corpus 144, 563). This is a statement about ONE
         and TWO blank lines: a run of THREE OR MORE is CARVE-P9-014's hard
         boundary, so the marker below it is not a "next sibling marker" at
         all and there is no one list left to be loose. Tightness is a property of the
         WHOLE ITEM, not of an individual block: a tight item's paragraphs
         ALL render WITHOUT `<p>` (<li>text</li>), every one of them, not
         only the first, except as CARVE-P9-081 below states; a loose
         item's paragraphs are ALL wrapped (<li><p>text</p></li>). An item can hold more than one paragraph
         block and still be tight -- L2 and L6 below cover the ways that
         happens without a blank line ever appearing.
         When the first visible block of an item is a paragraph, it follows
         the opening `<li>` directly, whether the list is tight or loose and
         whether later blocks follow it. Later visible blocks start on their
         own indented lines.
         A TIGHT ITEM'S ATTRIBUTED PARAGRAPH KEEPS ITS `<p>` -- NORMATIVE
         [CARVE-P9-081]. In a tight item, a paragraph that carries block
         attributes renders as `<p>` with those attributes; the item stays
         tight and its other paragraphs stay bare. The attributes belong on
         the paragraph's own element, so they are neither dropped nor moved
         to the `<li>`. A description body that holds one paragraph does the
         same (CARVE-P9-067): an attributed paragraph renders as `<p>` inside
         the `<dd>`, and an unattributed one stays bare.

             - {.c}            ->  <ul><li><p class="c">text</p></li></ul>
               text

             :: T              ->  <dl><dt>T</dt><dd><p class="c">text</p></dd></dl>
             : {.c}
               text

      L1a THE ITEM'S FIRST BLOCK DOES NOT MATTER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-029]. L1 asks
         whether the item holds a blank-line-separated second PARAGRAPH. It
         does not ask what the FIRST block was, and nothing here depends on
         that. So

             - - a

               Body.

         is LOOSE: the sub-list is the item's first block, `Body.` is a
         second paragraph after a blank line, and L1 is satisfied. The same
         holds for every other lead -- plain text, a heading, an attribute
         block, a block quote -- all of which core rendering handles
         loose.

      L1b AN INVISIBLE LINE DOES NOT CANCEL THE SEPARATION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-030]. L1
         asks whether the item holds a blank-line-separated second PARAGRAPH.
         An INVISIBLE LINE (§10 I5, AN INVISIBLE LINE IS ONE CLASSIFICATION)
         emits no visible block, which is why it cannot BE the second one
         (L1a's argument), and by the same clause's property 3 it
         does not choose the next line's owner either: it is not a separator,
         so the separation it appears to interrupt is intact and the item is
         LOOSE. This clause is that property's consequence for list
         TIGHTNESS.

             - a

               %% n
               text

         is loose. `text` is a second paragraph, the blank line precedes it,
         and the comment renders nothing.

      L1c THE BLANK IS READ AT THE LIST'S LEVEL, NOT THE ITEM'S. L1's first
         disjunct asks what stands BETWEEN one item and the next sibling
         marker. A blank line with nothing of the item after it stands between
         them however the item's interior accounted for the line -- including
         where an unterminated container carries it as content and the output
         shows it doing so, as an unterminated code fence's empty payload line
         does. So

             - ```           - ::: d
               b               b

             - s             - s

         are both LOOSE, and so are the same documents with the closer
         written. The state of the container above the blank does not enter
         this question either, which is the same property PART 0 S2 already
         states for where the item ENDS.

         An INTERIOR fence blank does not loosen the list: content follows it
         before the sibling marker, so no blank line separates the items --

             - ```
               a

               b
               ```
             - c

      L2 COMPACT LIST BLOCKS -- Carve deviation from djot/CommonMark. A
         blank line before an item's sub-BLOCK (sub-list, block quote,
         fenced code, fenced div, heading, table, line block, definition
         list) does NOT loosen: the item
         stays tight, lead text inline, block attached. Only a genuine
         second PARAGRAPH (blank + indented plain prose), or a blank line
         BETWEEN items, loosens. The blank line is still required to START
         the block, so recognition and the uniformity principle are
         unchanged -- only the tight/loose RENDERING differs (djot renders
         these loose). Pinned by corpus compact-list-blocks.
      L3 CONTINUATION MARKER `+` -- Carve addition. PART 0 S2 and its `+`
         family entry define it (CARVE-P0-024). A lone `+`, on its own line or
         as the whole content of a marker line, offers one container its next
         block, parsed like any other. `- + text` keeps `+ text` as text. Quote
         prefixes select their quote level (`> +`, not `>+`). An accepted
         marker interrupts an open paragraph and its grant ends at the attached
         block's boundary.

         ONE ATTACH RULE FOR EVERY HOST -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-067]. Lists,
         quotes, definition bodies and footnote bodies all use this one test.
         The marker's host is the innermost opener on its marker line, or for
         a `+` on its own line the open container whose marker column it stands
         at. The marker attaches the next line's block when all of these hold:

         - that line is not blank, and a line of nothing but quote markers
           counts as blank;
         - it carries the prefixes of the quotes around the host, and a `>`
           left of a container holding that quote is not its prefix;
         - measured through those prefixes it stands at the host's marker
           column, and a `+` on its own line at the `+`'s own column;
         - it is not the host's sibling: a list marker under a list item, a
           `: ` or `:: ` line under a definition body;
         - it is not the closer of an open div.

         So a list attaches in every host except a list item, and a quote
         follower nests. No other column reaches: not the content column, not
         column 0 of a quote level, unless that is the marker column. The
         column of the `+` itself decides nothing else. Blank lines between
         the marker and its block refuse it. Invisible lines between the
         marker and the next line are skipped: they are read in the host, and
         the gates apply to the first visible line after them (carve#4449).
         Link, note and abbreviation definitions are one class here (CARVE-P9-013):
         an abbreviation definition the `+` skips defines in the document when
         the host stands in the document. Under a host in a quote, an item or a
         div the line is text (CARVE-P12-026), so it is the next line. An invisible line off the marker column is itself that
         next line, and a note definition's body belongs to the note. A blank line, the end of
         input or a sibling after them refuses the `+`. Examples, with `/` for
         a line break:

         - `- +` / `para` gives the item the paragraph; `:: t` / `: +` / `- l`
           gives the description the list; `[^a]: +` / `- l` gives the note the
           list; `> +` / `x` gives the quote `x`; `> +` / `> x` nests a quote
           holding `x`.
         - `- - +` / `  para` gives the inner item the paragraph, and
           `> [^b]: +` / `> # H` gives the note the heading.
         - `- +` / `  para` is refused and reads `<li>+` / `para</li>`. Write the
           block at the marker column, or drop the `+`.
         - `- a` / `  - +` / `para` is refused: the inner marker column is 2.
         - `> > +` / `# H` is refused: the line lacks the outer quote's prefix.
         - `- +` / `- b` is refused: `<li>+</li>` and a sibling item.
         - `- +` / `%% c` / `text` gives the item the paragraph, and a
           `%%%` comment fence reads the same. A `{% c %}` line is not one of
           these: it is a paragraph holding an inline comment (CARVE-P9-042),
           so the `+` attaches that paragraph, and `- +` / `{% c %}` / `# H`
           gives an empty `<li>` and a heading after the list.
           `- a` / `+` / `{x}` / `text`
           gives the item `<p x="">`. `- +` / `*[A]: b` / `text` gives the
           item `text` and defines `A`.
         - `- +` / `%% c` / blank / `text` is refused: `<li>+</li>` and a
           paragraph. So is `- +` / `%% c` / `- b`: `<li>+</li>` and a sibling
           item.
         - `- +` / `%% c` / `  text` is refused: the visible line stands at the
           content column. `- a` / `+` / `{x}` / `  - c` is refused the same
           way, and `{x}` labels the list outside the item (carve#4278).

         A `+` on the next line at the marker column is a marker for the same
         host. If it attaches, its block is the one the earlier marker offered
         and both markers are consumed: `- +` / `+` / `x` gives the item `x`.
         If it is refused it is a letter and is itself the attached block:
         `- +` / `+` / a blank line / `x` gives `<li>+</li>` and a paragraph
         after the list.

         A REFUSED `+` IS A PLAIN LETTER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-085].
         Rulings carve#3421 and carve#4396. It has no effect of its own: it
         closes only what that letter would close, it does not spend or block
         another marker, and a later line has the owner it would have after
         that letter. Replacing a refused `+` with a letter changes the output
         only in that character. So it folds into an open paragraph when that
         paragraph can continue, a following list marker reads as it does after
         that letter (it folds under an open paragraph unless it is a sibling,
         PART 0 S4), and otherwise the `+` starts a paragraph where a letter
         would. The `continuation-marker-refused`
         lint reports a recognized refusal.

         A `+` at a container's marker column is outside that container's code,
         raw or colon fence, and an accepted one ends that fence before the
         attached block begins. Separators inserted for attachment are not
         fence payload. A `+` at the fence's content column stays payload. A
         table `+ text` row follows §5 T6, rather than this rule.

      L4 CONTAINERS THAT TAKE THE MARKER: list items (`- +`, `1. +`, and task
         forms), definition bodies (`: +`), footnote bodies (`[^a]: +`, a body
         at §24 C3, CARVE-P9-083) and block quotes (`> +`). The marker and the
         rendered element differ; the attachment does not. Marker-line and
         separate-line forms take the same follower: `> a` / `+` / `- l` is a
         quote holding a paragraph and a list, and `> +` / `- l` a quote holding
         the list.

      L5 NOT CONTINUATION CONTAINERS (a lone `+` keeps its plain meaning):
         - fenced containers (admonition / generic div / line block /
           fenced block quote `::: >`) --
           their bodies already hold flush-left blocks; a lone `+` inside
           is literal text;
         - TABLE -- a `+` as a row's first non-indent character is the
           continuation ROW (§5 T6), a
           distinct construct;
         - LEAF blocks (heading, thematic break, code block, raw block,
           paragraph) hold no child blocks; a heading is a bounded title.
      L6 A COLLECTED DEFINITION LEAVES NO TRACE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-034]
         (PART 9R R1/R2 collection). Apply §10 I5 property 2 to collected link
         and footnote definitions: pass 1 registers them in the shared symbol
         table and pass 2 resolves uses document-wide. Their block-level host
         renders no placeholder paragraph, blank line or residual whitespace.
         This applies to list items, quotes, footnote bodies, definition
         descriptions, continuation-attached blocks and any future container
         admitting block content.

         "No trace" describes output, not block structure. I5 still interrupts
         the open paragraph without a blank, so following plain text starts a
         new block. L1/L2 govern its rendering: without a loosening blank the
         item stays tight and every paragraph, including the new one, renders
         bare. A definition between two item paragraphs therefore leaves two
         bare paragraph blocks; it neither merges them nor loosens the item.

      L7 ONE CONSUMED BOOLEAN SPELLS THE LOOSENESS NO BLANK LINE CAN --
         NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-035]. A container's preceding BLOCK-ATTRIBUTE LINE
         (§15) MAY carry the boolean `loose`. It says that the container's
         children render as BLOCKS rather than as inline runs, and it is
         CONSUMED: it never reaches the output as an HTML attribute. The
         precedent is PART 12 §15's `header-rows`, which rides the same line,
         carries a structural fact as a boolean, and is likewise consumed rather
         than emitted.

             {loose}
             - Note text.

         renders

             <ul>
               <li><p>Note text.</p></li>
             </ul>

         THE KEY IS NOT LIST-SPECIFIC. It applies to lists, definition lists,
         and any future container with a tight/loose axis. Cells and captions
         have no wrapped alternative; quotes, divs, line blocks and footnote
         bodies always wrap. On a container without the axis, `loose` is an
         ordinary boolean rendering `loose=""`.

         WHAT IS UNSPELLABLE WITHOUT IT. A one-item list can need paragraph
         wrapping without an inter-item blank. A definition description with
         one block cannot spell wrapping with a blank between entries; such a
         blank never loosens a definition list, at any entry count.

         THERE IS NO `tight` TWIN. Tightness is always spellable by removing
         blanks; this key supplies only the otherwise unspellable loose case.

         THE NAME IS THE TERM OF ART. `loose` names rendering, not the tree's
         block-node shape, and applies across HTML, Markdown, plain and ANSI.

         REDUNDANT USE IS A NO-OP. The key is legal on an already-loose list
         or a description already holding two blocks and changes no rendering.

         The canonical writer emits `loose` only when omitting it changes
         the re-parsed tree (CARVE-P11-006). On a definition list it always
         emits an authored `loose`, because that field is authored-only
         (PART 12 §8).

         ORDERED AND NESTED CONTAINERS TAKE THE SAME KEY AT THE SAME PLACEMENT.
         `{loose}` before an ordered list loosens it; at a sublist's indent
         it loosens the sublist, not the parent.

         A BOOLEAN AND AN EMPTY VALUE ARE ONE KEY; A VALUED ONE IS NOT.
         Consume `{loose}` and `{loose=""}` alike (PART 4). `loose=x` remains
         an ordinary attribute rendering `loose="x"`, without partial effect.

         EACH CONTAINER CARRIES THE KEY'S EFFECT IN ITS OWN FIELD. A list sets
         required `list.tight` false. A loosened DEFINITION LIST sets `definition_list.loose`
         as an authored-only field (PART 12 §8); otherwise its `<dd>` wrapping
         is derived from description block count. PART 12 §6 round-trips both.

   ============================================================================ *)
